00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:01.840
And part of the deal.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.240
Yes, that was a game over. Yeah.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:09.140
Part of the deal with your uncle was like, you also have to like prove you can like hold.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.200
Hold down a relationship.

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.960
Yeah. Like hold like a healthy, long lasting relationship and have the money.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:24.620
Right. So if you don't do those things, if you, if you just spend the whole time buying stocks, which is almost impossible because it doesn't really eat up anytime.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:35.060
Um, if you just use the clock, uh, to continue to skip time instead of paying attention to your girlfriend or, um, contributing to your health with exercise.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.940
What a life lesson. Yeah.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.000
Then if you don't do those things, those, that that's how you get game over.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.730
So the game overs are threefold.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:51.550
You can either have a take really bad care of your health, lose your girlfriend or, uh, not meet the money requirements or go bankrupt.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.050
Really? Yeah. If you're really playing this game poorly.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:08:02.850
So the rest of the game though, if we just take out the life simulator parts, which are really just broken down into three choices, right?

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:09.410
You get to like, you guys said, take your girlfriend to the carnival, take your girlfriend shopping or, uh, what was the other one?

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:11.610
Uh, uh, picnic.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:15.130
Picnic. Yeah. So you get to only choose those three things.

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:16.750
Then that's like, that's it.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.450
It's not like, then you have to do the picnic mini game.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.370
Yeah, no, you just choose it.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:22.370
It just happens.

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:38.230
And, and, and because it happens, the only thing, the only way they gamified that is that the morning of in the newspaper, there will be a story potentially about like some kind of crime that happened, uh, you know, on the streets or whatever.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:42.470
And so that's a cue to like, well, I shouldn't suggest we go to the carnival.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.890
Oh, I didn't think that at all.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:52.450
I thought that was supposed to, that was telling me like, oh, don't buy whatever company that's sort of like a hint towards.

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:53.930
Yeah. You wish it was like that.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:09:04.690
I thought that initially, and it wasn't until right now that it was confirmed to me that like, I realized what you were saying, like that it's like, yeah, it'll say like the, there's been sharks in the water or whatever.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.930
So like, okay, don't go swimming when you want to do your exercises.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:13.410
I realized that, but I wasn't sure if some of the headlines still had to do with like which stocks to buy or not.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.930
And it sounds like they don't, they don't.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.310
They don't. Yeah. Not the headlines themselves.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:32.130
It's just like, oh, you know, uh, you know, this kinds of, this kind of stock, like blue chip stock did well, but the others didn't are like, oh, the fed raised interest rates by 0.5%, which is like, you know, something you might hear nowadays, but didn't happen for the last 20.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:36.330
So it just like silly stuff like that is in the newspaper.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:41.550
And then it sort of picks the five top stocks from the last days trading.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:48.990
And so you can kind of like, you can use that to tell you like, oh, what should I pick going forward?

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:52.590
And there's some funny stocks.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:10:02.990
Yeah, but it is weird that, um, you know, if the whole rest of the game is just based on buying a stock at a certain price or buying multiple stocks, right?

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:09.510
Multiple shares of different companies and watching their values fluctuate, deciding when it is time to sell.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.110
It is weird that the game communicates to the player.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:19.690
So obviously via the newspaper, which stocks are going to do well that week.

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:28.470
Like, like I was surprised to find out that it wasn't like a, this is the, this was the trend and now the trend is going to change and you have to try to guess the trend.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:36.470
But instead, if they tell you blue chip stocks are on the rise, you should sell everything else and buy blue chip.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:40.610
So that doesn't even require any thought on the player for like speculation.

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:47.370
It's just, well, the newspaper told me that these stocks were on the rise, that these, you know, this group of stocks was on the rise.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:56.810
And if you know which ones are the blue chips or which ones are the cyclical or the growth stocks, then you might as well just buy the lowest priced one.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:03.650
So you can get in the most amount of shares so that as they grow up from Monday to Friday, you gain the highest return.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:08.590
Uh, I, I just can't believe that they would telegraph it like that.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.150
That would almost be like if, uh, to take a different game, right?

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:17.870
If Dracula and Castlevania told you, uh, you know, I'm about to turn into a bat.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.630
Like, so this way you prepare for that.

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:21.130
Right. And like, I'm about to punch you.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.670
Like they don't, they don't telegraph in that way.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:25.510
